ions  and with slides we have log n plus k log  log n  now in the distributional setting the  login  is replaced by the entropy  and in this paper we showed that in the  distribution  setting with slice we can further  replace  the log log n with a quantity that we  term  h2 of a distribution  which is just as sum over all  probabilities i  of the probability mu i times  log log 1 over mu i  and this matches  uh uh the same guarantee as in the  deterministic setting  if we take mu to be the uniform  distribution  over the elements  now we have both upper and lower bound  that  match with sharp constants  up to lower order terms  okay so so this was the result  uh on on  a 20 questions game and we further want  to say that this is computed  efficiently and using a binary search  like  algorithm that uses comparison queries  and for this reason we can apply  this result to uh sorting  with comparison queens  and here we have a setting where we  have a sorting algorithm that has  comparison queries  but at most k of them can  actually give you the wrong answer  and we further assume that there is some  prior  distribution over the possible  permutations  and that the permutation is drawn  from this distribution  for example we can assume that the  array is already  closed to be sorted  so in this setting we know some prior  over over there possible permutations  and here we can show that  the analog enter that  comes in the deterministic setting  can be replied by the entropy  of the set of possible permutations  so after discussing the sorting  application  we move to the proof so first we discuss  the upper bound  and then we move to the lower bound  so in the upper bound we show that for  the 20 questions game  there is a strategy of alice that us  at most the entropy plus  k h2 of the distribution  questions on expectation  and first we present the  an algorithm which is a binary search  algorithm  for the distribution distributional  setting  with no lies and this  is due to gilbert and more from 59  and let's see how this binary search  algorithm works  so uh it places each element  x i on the segment zero one  at some point p i  then it performed a binary search to  find  uh the point corresponding to the  element  and the binary search is very simple  first it asks whether the point  is greater than 0.5  let's say that bob says no and i'll  continue  whether it's greater than 0.25  no whether it's greater than 1 8  and let's say here that bob says yes and  now we know  that the object must  be x2 that's light in point  p2  so now that we've shown how to perform  this binary research  i would also like to explain how to play  the elements on the segment  so the high level idea is that  elements with higher probability  uh we should ask less questions in order  to reveal them  and that's because we are interested in  the expected number of questions  so we are interested more on minimizing  the questions for the  more probable elements  and for this reason element with higher  probability should be  more space around in the interval  because the more spacing that they have  the faster it takes to find them  in particular for each element i  we will create an interval  of length mu i namely the length  is the probability  and then we will place the  element in the middle of its  corresponding  interval now it  is very easy to show that  the number of questions that are  required to locate  each element i is at most  log one over its probability plus two  now taking an expectation over all the  elements  you get that the expected number of  questions  is at about the entropy plus two  and this is already very very close to  optimal  so we showed a binary search algorithm  that is close to the optimal algorithm  in the setting of no lines  now let's  explain how we can locate  and correct lies  so first we give some example and assume  that  the true element is x7 line in point p7  and that the first question uh  is answered in a lie by bob  so bob said that p7 is less than  one-half and  now notice the following property  in each consecutive question  p7 will be greater  than the separator so the true answer  to all consecutive questions should be  greater than  so it is greater than one quarter yes  it is greater than 0.375 yes  is greater than 0.43 yes  so now if bob doesn't lie we will see  many many consecutive greater than  answer  and we use this property to  locate suspicious question answers  and correct them  and let's first give the first example  for a suspicious  uh answer so let's say the answer was  less than  and all consecutive  answers were greater than  and many of them  now and this looks very suspicious uh to  be a lie  and let's look at another example so  remember that bob can in fact lie  even after uh his first live  so uh we can there can be  a k minus one additional lies  after uh the the first slide  and any uh sequence of a lesson answer  followed by many greater than answers  and at most k minus 1  lesson answers will also be  suspicious  and now let's look at the non-suspicion  uh  non-suspicious case and here we have a  lesson answer  followed by uh more  than a k lesson answers  in this setting uh the  marked lesson answer must  be correct because otherwise  uh there couldn't be so many a lesson  answer that follow this answer  so here we have  some intuition on how to find the  suspicious answers and now  i will give you a sketch of the  algorithm that uses  this intuition so first it executes  uh the binary search on the line as  a gilbert moore algorithm that i  described to you before and we  do this until we  find uh until we encounter  a suspicious answer  so here we have a suspicious answer  which is a lesson  followed by many greater than  and not so many other lesson answers  and once we find a suspicious answer  we remove all the  subsequent answers  and we verify this answer  by asking it 2k plus 1 times  and then we take the majority of  all the repeated  time that we asked this question  and the majority must be correct because  bob  can lie at most k times  then we continue uh  up to the point that either we find  another suspicious answer or  until the binary search  terminates  and once it terminates it returns some  object and we want to verify that this  is the correct object  and we do this again by asking 2k plus  2k plus 1 times whether this is the true  object  and if the answer is positive then we  just  output this object we found it and  otherwise we also have to delete some  questions and keep going  so now the analysis claimed that  the number of questions uh deleted  due to a suspicious answers  is not very high  and in this way  we get our final bound  on the performance of the algorithm  so that's it for the upper bound and now  let's move to the lower bound  and here we want to show that alice asks  at least the entropy plus  k h2 of mu questions on average  and before explaining the lower bound  with uh lies we start with the lower  bound with no lies  and here we use some lemma from  information theory that states that  in order to reveal a random variable  that is distributed  according to some distribution mu  we need to communicate at least  the entropy of mu bits of information  on average now  because at most one bit  of information is communicated in each  question then the number of questions  has to be at least  the entropy of the distribution  so that is a very simple uh lower bound  in uh in the setting of no  lines and now let's move to the vetting  with light  and here we want a larger  lower bound  so uh in this so uh  what we show that in this case  once alice reveals the element  x she also reveals  the locations of the lies  and we denote by y the vector of  the positions of the lines of bob  and because alice learns not only x but  also why  the number of bits that had to be  communicated to her  is at least the joint entropy between  x and between y  which is the vector of lies  and if we compute this quantity  we get exactly what we want  and there is a very short proof in our  paper so that's it for the lower bound  and now let's make a conclusion so we  had an  optimal algorithm for 20 questions with  slides  that is using comparison queries and it  is  computationally efficient a matching  lower bound and an application to  a noisy starting thanks for listening

